[38;5;231m⏺[39m One more fork before I sketch the design: [1mhow the module gets loaded.[0m Right now [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a classic [38;5;153m<script>[39m with
  everything in global scope, while [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m is CommonJS for Node. A shared module has to pick one, and this
  decision is baked into every consumer afterward.

  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153mvalidation.js[39m uses [38;5;153mexport[39m, [38;5;153mindex.html[39m switches to [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js">[39m. Standard, and
    the same file can be imported by a Node test runner. Cost: [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m is fetched under CORS rules, so opening
    [38;5;153mindex.html[39m from [38;5;153mfile://[39m stops working — you'd need [38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m or similar to view it locally.
  - [1mClassic script + global[0m — [38;5;153mvalidation.js[39m assigns to a global (e.g. [38;5;153mwindow.Validation[39m), loaded by a second [38;5;153m<script>[39m
    tag before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m. [38;5;153mfile://[39m keeps working with zero tooling. Cost: global namespace, and testing in Node needs a shim
    since there's no export.
  - [1mCommonJS, shared with [38;5;153msrc/[0m — matches [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m, and Node tests it directly with no config. Cost: browsers can't
    load CommonJS, so [38;5;153mapp.js[39m would need a bundler — a build step this repo doesn't have.

  I recommend ES modules. It's the only one of the three where the exact same file runs in the browser and under a test
  runner with no shim and no bundler, and given "general prep," testability is most of the value you're buying. The
  [38;5;153mfile://[39m loss is real but small — and I'd note it in the README as part of the change.
